ChatGPT said:

Excellent question 👍

Right now your app is bound to :3000, so you must specify the port in the URL.
If you want the URL to be just:

https://vendex-uat.company.com


(without :3000), you have two options:

🔹 Option 1: Run your Spring Boot app on port 443

443 is the default HTTPS port, so browsers don’t show it.

In application.properties:

server.port=443


Make sure your process has permission to bind to low ports (<1024).
On Linux you can either:

Run as root (not recommended)

Or use:

sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' $(readlink -f $(which java))


👉 This lets Java bind to port 443 without root.

🔹 Option 2: Use a reverse proxy (preferred in production)

Keep your Spring Boot app on port 3000.

Use Apache HTTPD or Nginx to listen on 443 and forward requests to 3000.

Example (Apache config):

<VirtualHost *:443>
    ServerName vendex-uat.company.com

    SSLEngine on
    SSLCertificateFile /etc/ssl/certs/vendex-uat.crt
    SSLCertificateKeyFile /etc/ssl/private/vendex-uat.key

    ProxyPreserveHost On
    ProxyPass / http://localhost:3000/
    ProxyPassReverse / http://localhost:3000/
</VirtualHost>


Then your users hit:

https://vendex-uat.company.com


Apache forwards internally to http://localhost:3000.

✅ Best practice:
Keep Spring Boot on 3000 (or 8080/8443), and put a reverse proxy (Apache/Nginx/F5) in front.
That way you don’t need root privileges, and you can also handle SSL termination, logging, load balancing, etc.

